menu "Observore"

config OBSERVORE_MDNS_HOSTNAME
    string "mDNS hostname"
    default "observore"
    help
        The device answers to this name plus ".local" on whichever network it
        is joined to.

        mDNS is link-local multicast and does not cross subnets, so a device on
        an isolated IoT VLAN will not answer clients on the main LAN unless the
        router is set to reflect mDNS between them.  Observore is also only on
        the network during its uplink window, so the name resolves then and not
        while it patrols.

config OBSERVORE_LED_GPIO
    int "User LED GPIO"
    default 21
    help
        The XIAO ESP32S3 user LED is GPIO21 and is active low.

config OBSERVORE_BUTTON_GPIO
    int "Mode button GPIO"
    default 0
    help
        GPIO0 is the BOOT button on the XIAO ESP32S3, active low.  Holding it
        while running toggles the console SoftAP; it only enters download mode
        when held across a reset, so reusing it here is safe.

comment "Wi-Fi uplink -- leave blank here and set it with menuconfig or NVS"

config OBSERVORE_WIFI_SSID
    string "Home network SSID (optional)"
    default ""
    help
        Leave this EMPTY in sdkconfig.defaults, which is a tracked file.  Set
        it with `idf.py menuconfig` (it lands in sdkconfig, which is
        gitignored), or with a gitignored credentials.conf, or at runtime from
        the console -- which stores it in NVS and never touches the repo at
        all.  A credential set at runtime takes precedence over this one.

        tools/check_no_secrets.sh fails if a value ends up in a tracked file.

config OBSERVORE_WIFI_PASSWORD
    string "Home network password (optional)"
    default ""
    help
        Empty means an open network.  Same warning as the SSID above: never
        commit a real value.

config OBSERVORE_WIFI_AUTOJOIN
    bool "Join the configured network at boot"
    default y
    help
        When credentials are configured, join at boot so the console is
        reachable on your LAN without the SoftAP.

        This costs detection: the radio has one channel, so while associated
        the Wi-Fi sniffer is suspended and only BLE scanning and AP scans
        continue.  Turn this off to stay on full patrol and reach the uplink
        only on demand.  If the network cannot be joined, Observore falls back to
        patrol rather than sitting associated to nothing.

config OBSERVORE_PATROL_WINDOW_S
    int "Seconds spent patrolling before visiting the uplink"
    default 120
    range 20 3600
    help
        Observore is built to sit in one place, and all Wi-Fi detection -- the
        access-point scan and the promiscuous sniff both -- runs only while
        patrolling.  A device parked permanently on the uplink is a BLE-only
        detector, which gives up Remote ID drone and hidden-AP detection
        entirely.  So it alternates: patrol for this long, then join the
        network briefly to push what it found.

config OBSERVORE_UPLINK_WINDOW_S
    int "Seconds spent on the uplink before returning to patrol"
    default 30
    range 10 3600
    help
        Long enough to flush queued notifications.  The window is held open
        while somebody is actually reading the console, so browsing is never
        cut off mid-page.

config OBSERVORE_UPLINK_MAX_S
    int "Hard ceiling on one uplink visit (seconds)"
    default 180
    range 30 3600
    help
        The uplink window is extended while the console is being read, and the
        page polls every two seconds -- so a browser tab left open would keep
        the device on the uplink indefinitely, where it does no Wi-Fi detection
        at all.  Past this, it returns to patrol regardless.  A user interface
        must not be able to blind the detector.

config OBSERVORE_CONSOLE_IDLE_S
    int "Seconds of console silence before the uplink window may close"
    default 45
    range 5 600

config OBSERVORE_UPLINK_RETRY_S
    int "Backoff after a failed uplink join (seconds)"
    default 300
    range 30 3600
    help
        A join that fails costs the connect timeout, so after a failure the
        next attempt waits this long rather than retrying every patrol window
        and spending a fifteen-second stall each time.  A successful join
        resumes the ordinary alternation.

config OBSERVORE_UPLINK_GRACE_S
    int "Seconds to tolerate a dropped uplink before patrolling"
    default 45
    range 5 600
    help
        Brief drops are normal and reconnect on their own.  Past this, staying
        in uplink mode means the Wi-Fi sniffer is off and there is no network
        either, so patrolling is strictly better.

config OBSERVORE_WIFI_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S
    int "Uplink connect timeout (seconds)"
    default 15
    range 5 60

config OBSERVORE_BLE_SCAN_INTERVAL_MS
    int "BLE scan interval (ms)"
    default 160
    range 20 1000

config OBSERVORE_BLE_SCAN_WINDOW_MS
    int "BLE scan window (ms)"
    default 60
    range 10 1000
    help
        Must not exceed the interval.  BLE and Wi-Fi share one radio and the
        coexistence arbiter divides it by this duty cycle, sharply and
        non-linearly: at a 100% duty cycle the Wi-Fi sniffer receives almost
        nothing at all (2 frames in 40 s, measured), while 37.5% still yields
        around 200.  The default trades roughly a third of BLE throughput for
        a sniffer that functions.

config OBSERVORE_AP_SSID_PREFIX
    string "Console SoftAP SSID prefix"
    default "console"
    help
        The last three bytes of the MAC are appended.  The default deliberately
        does not advertise what the device is.

config OBSERVORE_AP_PASSWORD
    string "Console SoftAP password"
    default "observore-console"
    help
        Must be at least 8 characters for WPA2; a shorter value leaves the
        SoftAP open.  Change it.

endmenu
